    altTranslation: "Like sharp young neem-buds your pointed white teeth,\nyour ear-edges pale, red-mouthed, going through\nthe great desolate ravined waste, driving with raised hands\nyour long-bannered chariot up the high forest path — you!\nThough you may come that far, you have not come here.\nMy lovely breasts marked with desire-spots grow pale —\nyou parted from me, and I cannot endure it.\nIs THIS what you called sweetness, my lord?",
    note: "A pālai poem of bitter complaint. The heroine (tālaivi) has been left at home while her lover has gone on a journey — perhaps to seek wealth, perhaps for warfare. She knows or suspects he is near but not approaching her. The closing line — 'Is THIS what you called sweetness?' — is a piercing reproach that recurs throughout Sangam akam poetry: the lover's earlier promises set against his current absence. The poet Irumpiṭart-talaiyār is also represented in the Puṟanāṉūṟu (poem 3, in praise of Karikāl)."
