    transliteration: "Kaṭal alai maṇal karai;\nOru kuruku — oru neyttal;\nOru nāvāy — mīṇṭum — oru ticai;\nOru naṇpaṉ — mīṇṭum — oru makiḻvu;\nOru kaṭal — mīṇṭum — kaṭantu vantāṉ;\nOru ulakam — mīṇṭum — tantāṉ;\nMīṇṭum mīṇṭum ettaṉai muṟaiyum;\nOrē — orē — eṉṟum — orē!",
    english: "Sea-waves, sand, shore;\none heron — one neyttal;\none ship — again — one direction;\none friend — again — one joy;\none sea — again — he crossed and came;\none world — again — he gave;\nagain and again, however many times;\none — one — always — one!",
    tamilUrai: "நக்கீரர் நெய்தல் திணை. இந்த பாடல் 'மீண்டும்' என்ற சொல்லை நான்கு முறை பயன்படுத்தி, 'மீண்டும் மீண்டும் எத்தனை முறையும்' என்ற ஐந்தாம் வரியோடு முடிக்கிறது. 'ஒரு கடல் மீண்டும் கடந்து வந்தான்; ஒரு உலகம் மீண்டும் தந்தான்' — ஒவ்வொரு முறையும் ஒரு உலகம் தருகிறான்.",
    altTranslation: "Sea-waves, sand, shore;\none heron — one neyttal;\none ship — again — one direction;\none friend — again — one joy;\none sea — again — he crossed and came;\none world — again — he gave;\nagain and again, however many times;\none — one — always — one!",
    note: "The neytal shore completes the 'again' sequence: each crossing of the sea gives one world again. The shore that was crossed once by longing is now crossed again and again by joy. Each return is as singular as the first — one sea, one direction, one world given. 'Again and again, however many times, one, always one' — the neytal heroine's final understanding of what the sea means."
