    altTranslation: "Mountain rain, kōṅku — the rains;\nthe vēṅkai bees swarm;\nhe came — again — one mind;\nhe brought one garland — stretched one hand;\nhe showed one smile — he spoke one word;\nhe gave one heart — he built one world;\nagain and again he will come — as one;\none love — one — always!",
    note: "The phrase 'again and again he will come, one' (mīṇṭum mīṇṭum varuvāṉ — orē) distills the entire 'again' sequence: repetition and singularity are not opposites. He comes again and again, and each time he comes as one — whole, complete, singular. Repetition does not fragment; love renews itself whole each time. This is the kuṟiñci counter to the marutam's cycle of grief."
