    altTranslation: "Rain-mountain kōṅku — the rains;\none woman, one eye, one world;\nyāḻ, sea, mountain — with you — will endure;\nagain and again — anew — with you;\none love — yāḻ, sea, mountain — all;\none night — one mountain — with you;\none I, one you — with you — will endure;\nthe gods — yāḻ, sea, mountain — with you!",
    note: "The gods themselves become 'with you': they are yāḻ-sea-mountain, present with the beloved. This transforms the divine witness from observer to participant — the gods are not watching from outside but inhabiting the love itself, as music, depth, and firmness. Ammūvaṉār takes the divine witness invocation further than Kapilar: not just 'let the gods know' but 'the gods are yāḻ-sea-mountain, with you.'"
