    altTranslation: "The paddy-field — like a yāḻ — resounds;\nthe lotus tank — like a sea — deep;\nlike a mountain — endures — one heart;\nyāḻ, sea, mountain — not there — one husband;\none courtesan — with you — she called;\none house — yāḻ, sea, mountain — of solitude;\none woman — yāḻ, sea, mountain — of dream;\none hope — yāḻ, sea, mountain — always!",
    note: "Pēyaṉār ends with a remarkable marutam declaration: 'one hope, yāḻ-sea-mountain, always.' The hope is not small or fragile — it is as vast and enduring as the anthology's greatest metaphors. The wife's heart endures like a mountain even when the husband does not; her hope is oceanic even when the household is desolate. The marutam tradition honours this hope as its own kind of greatness."
